Age-Related Macular Degeneration (AMD)
Opthalmology & ENT
Central vision loss, Metamorphopsia, Macular drusen

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-003
Reason for Visit: Sudden distressing deterioration of central vision and high fall risk in an elderly patient.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult daughter
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed acute, painless loss of central vision and distortion of straight lines. This necessitated an urgent home assessment to exclude immediate sight-threatening emergencies such as acute stroke or retinal detachment, and to manage the resulting acute anxiety and severe immediate fall risk prior to standard ophthalmology availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "When I look straight at the door frame, it looks wavy, and there is a dark grey patch right in the middle of my sight."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a progressive but recently exacerbated painless loss of central high-resolution vision. Now experiencing marked metamorphopsia (straight lines appearing wavy) and a central scotoma (dark patch) in the visual field. Notes difficulty reading fine print and recognizing faces. No eye pain, flashing lights, or curtain-like peripheral vision loss.
Functional Status: Previously independent; currently requiring standby assist for ambulation due to visual uncertainty.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk due to compromised depth perception. Daughter staying overnight to assist.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 | HR: 78 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Anxious, navigating the room tentatively with hands outstretched.
Physical Exam: Visual acuity significantly decreased bilaterally, worse in the right eye, not correctable with pinhole. Confrontation visual fields demonstrate intact peripheral vision but a distinct central defect (scotoma) in the right eye. Amsler grid testing confirms severe metamorphopsia. Direct ophthalmoscopy reveals yellowish subretinal nodules (drusen) and pigmentary changes in the macular region. Pupils equal and reactive to light; no relative afferent pupillary defect. Neurological exam grossly intact.
Environmental Observations: Poorly lit hallways, loose rugs in the living area constituting severe trip hazards.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient on antihypertensives; compliant.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Age-Related Macular Degeneration (AMD) - Status: Exacerbated (Suspected conversion to Wet AMD)
Differentials:

Acute Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Intact peripheral vision, isolated central ocular pathology, and complete absence of focal neurological deficits (motor/sensory/speech).

Retinal Detachment - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of photopsia (flashing lights), floaters, or a peripheral curtain-like shadow over the visual field.

Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Painless presentation, clear corneas, and normally reactive pupils.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Fall risk, poor lighting)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute-on-chronic decline in central vision accompanied by profound metamorphopsia. While AMD is a chronic condition, the sudden reported change raised immediate red flags for acute retinal pathology or cerebrovascular event. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary to perform a basic neurological and ophthalmoscopic exam, successfully ruling out stroke and retinal detachment. The visit was critical to establishing environmental safety, preventing a catastrophic fall in a suddenly visually impaired patient during the after-hours period, and arranging expedited specialist ophthalmology review for suspected wet AMD.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Urgent referral generated for an Ophthalmologist assessment within 48-72 hours for formal optical coherence tomography (OCT) and potential anti-VEGF therapy.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce fall risk, immediately remove all loose rugs and install bright, motion-sensor nightlights. This environmental modification yields immediate positive reinforcement by allowing the patient to navigate safely using their preserved peripheral vision, reducing anxiety.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Aged Care Assessment Team (ACAT) referral for home safety modifications.
DME/Safety (Optional): Prescription of a four-wheeled walker for stability during the acute visual transition.
Follow-up: GP review in 3 days to confirm ophthalmology engagement.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if sudden total loss of vision occurs, new flashes of light/floaters appear, or any sudden limb weakness or speech difficulty develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal ophthalmological imaging (OCT).
Clinical Course: Condition requires urgent specialist assessment. Dry AMD progresses slowly, but the acute wavy lines suggest wet AMD, which requires rapid treatment to preserve remaining central vision.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not drive under any circumstances. If you experience a sudden black curtain coming over your vision or any stroke-like symptoms, call 000 immediately.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The wavy lines suggest fluid or bleeding behind the retina (wet AMD). A specialist can inject medication to stop this bleeding and preserve your sight.
Actionable Steps: Rely on peripheral vision for navigation. Daughter to remove all trip hazards tonight. Keep all pathways brightly lit.

